## Goal
Prove that TPF agentic behaviour emerges from ordinary v3 composition rather than a second Agent runtime.

The reference must use the architecture actually landed in #632/#634:

```text
ApplicationState
    ↓
LLM Query — exactly one inference
    ↓
Decision
    ├── call:<tpf.llm.AgentCall>
    │       ↓
    │   operation.mode: dynamic
    │       ↓
    │   native Query | Command
    │       ↓
    │   <tpf.connector.OperationObservation>
    │       ↓
    │   application-authored state transition
    │       ↓
    │   ApplicationState
    │       ↓
    │   pipeline:self
    │
    └── complete:ApplicationResult
            ↓
         terminal
```

> **The LLM decides once. TPF invokes one configured capability once. The application interprets the observation. Recursive pipeline composition creates agentic behaviour.**

## Dependencies
Depends on:
- #630 contributed protocol types;
- #624 named connector bindings;
- #576 provider-backed QueryOperation integration;
- #632 one-turn LLM Query + binding-aware `<tpf.llm.AgentCall>`;
- #634 dynamic operation selection + `<tpf.connector.OperationObservation>`;
- landed bounded direct self-recursive `pipeline:` composition.

#633 runtime snapshot identity is not required for this static/release-pinned proof.

## Reference shape
Use one small domain-neutral application with:
- application-authored State and Result;
- closed Decision union: `call:<tpf.llm.AgentCall> | complete:Result`;
- one LLM Query step with an explicit release-pinned callable catalogue;
- at least one callable Query and one callable Command on named connector bindings;
- one dynamic operation-selection step using the #634 `operation.mode: dynamic` mechanics — **not** `kind: dispatch`;
- `<tpf.connector.OperationObservation>` as the generic execution result;
- an ordinary application Mapper/service/reducer that folds the observation into State;
- recursive `ONE_TO_ONE` `pipeline:self` continuation;
- bounded recursion/turn budget.

The state-transition step is important: #634 intentionally does not manufacture application state from a dynamically typed operation result.

## Semantics to prove
- exactly one model inference occurs per Decide turn;
- the model proposes only binding + operation + canonical arguments through `AgentCall`;
- dynamic selection is compiler-owned control/binding mechanics, not a new semantic Connector operation kind;
- at most one selected operation is invoked per call branch;
- Query targets retain Query capture/replay/outcomes, including valid `NotFound` observations;
- Command targets retain durable effect identity, duplicate handling, policy/confirmation/recovery semantics;
- `OperationObservation` preserves trusted operation/outcome/result facts without becoming Agent state;
- application code owns observation interpretation/state evolution;
- the dispatcher never decides whether another turn is needed;
- recursion is the only iteration mechanism;
- completion remains ordinary typed terminal routing;
- no runtime snapshot subsystem is required for a release-pinned callable catalogue.

## Structured-output proof
Exercise the #632/#634 structured-output contract without adding new semantics:
- `REQUIRED` remains the default;
- optionally include one explicit `OPTIONAL` fixture/local-model proof if useful;
- no silent downgrade, repair loop or second inference.

## Non-goals
- MCP is not required;
- AskUser/Await is not required;
- no Agent execution ID/state machine/ledger;
- no universal AgentState/memory subsystem;
- no dynamic user/tenant/turn capability refresh requirement;
- no semantic `kind: dispatch`.

## Acceptance
A developer can inspect the YAML, generated metadata and runtime path and see the complete composition:

```text
State → LLM Query → AgentCall → dynamic native operation
      → OperationObservation → authored state transition
      → State → pipeline:self
```

or typed completion, with no hidden Agent loop/runtime and no weakening of native Query/Command guarantees.

## Observation basis

Validated on 2026-07-28 against the latest remote corrective line available before Nightwing:

- `wip/0.4.7-lcy-cleanup-deadlock-20260727`
- commit `e3841b3b2b42b891e0218277dc0113099fd185a5`

The branch contains 238 tracked Markdown/MDX/text artifacts:

- 89 below `docs/`;
- 43 below `backlog/`;
- 30 below `specs/`;
- 20 below `templates/`;
- 16 below `plugins/`;
- 11 at repository root; and
- 29 across harness, governance, guardrails, policies, roles, GitHub metadata, and telemetry.

The tree also contains nine byte-identical document pairs between `docs/spec-archive/2026-07-20-sentinel-recovery/` and `specs/2026-07-19-sprint-sentinel-epic/`, including the PRD, Spec, recovery, platform, scope, and acceptance-matrix material.

This is an inventory finding, not a claim that every counted file is unnecessary.

## Existing foundation

Issue #22 is complete and already provides the canonical artifact-topology contract, ADR-0045, compatibility inventory, lifecycle semantics, and a validator that can classify misplaced, duplicated, orphaned, stale, and unreferenced artifacts.

The repository also has:

- a documentation inventory in `governance/observation-doc-governance.json`;
- a short navigation page in `docs/README.md`;
- maintained, normative-record, compatibility-redirect, and review-candidate document classes; and
- a canonical Feature/Epic package layout below `specs/<feature-id>/`.

The remaining gap is not another topology design. It is a controlled migration and information-architecture cleanup of the actual documentation estate using that accepted foundation.

## Problem

The accumulated documentation exposes too many overlapping entry points, historical copies, version-specific readiness records, working specifications, prompts, templates, redirects, and maintained references to a user who only needs a small number of clear journeys.

A document may currently be valid as historical evidence while still being harmful as discoverable guidance. Exact duplicates and overlapping instructions make it difficult to answer:

- which document is authoritative now;
- which document is only historical evidence;
- which page a user should read next;
- whether two similar commands describe the same supported path;
- whether an old release/readiness page still applies; and
- whether an unlinked file is intentionally retained or simply orphaned.

Deleting files indiscriminately would destroy provenance. Keeping every text artifact equally visible preserves unnecessary complexity. A governed retirement pass is required.

## Outcome

Produce one deliberately small, role- and task-oriented documentation estate for users while preserving normative decisions, audit evidence, and historical provenance in clearly separated governed locations.

Every retained text artifact must have a declared purpose, audience, lifecycle, authority, and discoverable owner. Superseded or orphaned guidance must be redirected, archived, or removed through a previewable and reviewable migration rather than left as an accidental second source of truth.

## Scope

### 1. Complete text-artifact inventory

Inventory all tracked human-readable text artifacts, including Markdown, MDX, text files, prompt assets, templates, checklists, release/readiness records, active feature material, and archived evidence.

Classify every artifact as exactly one of:

- normative authority;
- maintained user journey;
- maintained task guide;
- maintained reference;
- reusable template or prompt;
- generated/project-state artifact;
- active working Feature/Epic artifact;
- historical or audit evidence;
- compatibility redirect;
- superseded pending retirement; or
- orphan/unknown requiring disposition.

Record audience, lifecycle, authority, replacement, retention reason, and referring paths.

### 2. Minimal user-facing information architecture

Define and enforce a concise public navigation model in which:

- the root README is the product and decision front door;
- setup/configuration has one supported task path;
- the end-to-end lifecycle has one user journey;
- the operating model remains the normative process authority;
- domain references are linked only when needed;
- historical evidence, active implementation material, and maintainer internals are not presented as ordinary user guidance; and
- compatibility pages redirect rather than repeat normative content.

This issue supplies the cleaned documentation estate consumed by #3; it does not duplicate #3's product-positioning work.

### 3. Conflict, duplication, and staleness analysis

Detect and disposition at least:

- byte-identical copies;
- semantically overlapping user instructions;
- conflicting commands or lifecycle order;
- stale version/release claims;
- duplicate normative statements;
- broken or circular navigation;
- references to superseded paths;
- release/readiness records presented as current guidance;
- unreferenced prompts, templates, and checklists; and
- active materia

## Summary

Nothing rejects a class hierarchy whose `extends` edges form a cycle. The declarations check clean, and the generated JavaScript throws before any user code runs.

## Repro

```esc
class A extends B {
    constructor(mut self) {}
}
class B extends A {
    constructor(mut self) {}
}
```

No diagnostic is reported. The emitted `build/lib/index.js` is:

```js
export class B extends A {
  constructor() {
    super();
  }
}
export class A extends B {
  constructor() {
    super();
  }
}
```

Importing the module throws:

```
ReferenceError: Cannot access 'A' before initialization
```

`B` names `A` in its own `extends` clause while `A` is still in the temporal dead zone. No emission order fixes it, since whichever class is written first names the other.

## Why it belongs in the checker

A cyclic hierarchy is meaningless rather than merely un-emittable. `A` would have to be a subtype of `B` and `B` of `A`, and each would inherit the other's members without either declaring a base. The solver's own walks already treat the cycle as something to survive rather than something legal: `constrainNominalWalk` and `inheritedElems` each carry a visited set purely to terminate on one.

So the fix is to report the cycle where `extends` is resolved, rather than to reorder or drop the clause in codegen.

## Suggested direction

When a class's `extends` edge is resolved, walk the chain from the new edge and report a diagnostic if it reaches the class being declared. `inheritedElems` in `internal/solver/classes.go` already performs that walk with the visited set the check needs. A cycle detected this way should drop the edge, the way `CannotExtendFinalClassError` does, so nothing downstream records a hierarchy that cannot exist.

## Notes

- Surfaced while implementing #859. Emitting the `extends` clause is what turns this from a silently wrong program into a load-time failure. Before that change the two classes were emitted standalone, so the cycle was invisible at runtime and each instance simply lacked its inherited members.
- The `final` check in `resolveClassSupers` is the closest existing precedent for rejecting an edge at resolution time.

